@TITLE@ QilinRecomp for macOS (Apple silicon and Intel)
==============================================================

@FULL_TITLE@ (@STUDIO@; Rhythm Heaven Fever in North America),
recompiled to run natively: the game's own program translated into C++ and
compiled for your Mac, drawn with Vulkan (through MoltenVK, which comes
with the app).

No game data is included. You need your own copy of the game: @RELEASE@,
extracted into a folder with Dolphin. Other regions and revisions (Rhythm
Heaven Fever, Minna no Rhythm Tengoku) are other programs and aren't
supported.


Your game folder
----------------
In Dolphin, right-click the game in the list, choose Properties, open the
Filesystem tab, right-click the disc at the top and choose Extract Entire
Disc, and pick an empty folder. The folder you need is the one holding
"sys" and "files": if Dolphin made a DATA folder inside the one you picked,
it is that DATA folder. A .wbfs, .iso or .rvz file can't be read as it is:
extract it first.


Playing
-------
1. Drag @TITLE@.app onto Applications (in this disk image's window).
   Building the game needs about 2 GB free.
2. Open @TITLE@ from Applications. The first time, macOS stops it, because
   the app isn't signed by an Apple developer account (see below):
   - open System Settings, then Privacy & Security;
   - scroll down to Security, where it says "@TITLE@ was blocked to
     protect your Mac" (or "... was blocked from use because it is not
     from an identified developer");
   - click Open Anyway, then Open Anyway again in the window that appears,
     and confirm with your password or Touch ID.
   macOS asks only once. (On macOS 14, right-clicking the app in Finder and
   choosing Open does the same.)
3. The game is compiled with Apple's own compiler, free from Apple (its
   licence doesn't let it come with this download). If your Mac doesn't
   have it yet, the app says so: click "Install Apple's command line
   tools", follow Apple's installer (a few minutes), then click "Check
   again".
4. Click "Choose your extracted disc" and pick the folder above, or drag it
   onto the window. It tells you straight away if it is the wrong game,
   another region or another revision, or if part of the disc is missing.
5. Wait for the first build: a few minutes. Your game files never leave
   your Mac.
6. Click Play. The window hides while the game runs.

After that, the app remembers your folder, and Play starts the game
straight away. After an update it builds the game again first, recompiling
only what changed. Leave the disc folder where it is: the game reads its
files from it while you play.

The game's output is in run.log and run-errors.log, and the build's in
build.log, all in ~/Library/Application Support/qilin/@GAME@/work (in
Finder: Go, Go to Folder). Send them when reporting a problem. If the
picture stays black after the first screen, close the game and start it
again (a rare fault at start-up, not yet found).

About "Open Anyway": the app is signed only by itself ("ad hoc"), not by
an Apple developer account, so Gatekeeper can't name who made it. Check
that you downloaded it from the project's GitHub releases page (below)
before you open it. Nothing in it changes that check or works around it.

Needs: macOS 14.5 (Sonoma) or newer, on an Apple silicon or Intel Mac, and
Apple's command line tools from Xcode 16 or newer, which need that macOS
(the app checks; older tools are updated with System Settings, General,
Software Update). Graphics go through MoltenVK, Vulkan over Apple's Metal.


Controls
--------
A Wii Remote held upright, buttons only (no motion controls).

  Controller               Keyboard                     Wii Remote
  south button (Xbox A),   A, Right Shift, Right        A
  right shoulder           arrow, Space
  east button (Xbox B),    B, Left Shift, Left arrow    B (the trigger)
  left shoulder, right
  trigger
  west, north buttons      1, 2                         1, 2
  Start, Back              Return; Delete or Tab        +; -
  Guide                    H                            HOME
  d-pad, left stick        I J K L, Up and Down arrows  the d-pad

On the title screen, press A and B together. Every connected controller
plays, and rumble reaches them.

F1 or Esc (a controller: Back and Start together): the settings menu (the
game pauses), with the controls (set by pressing them), the picture (size,
a CRT look, internal resolution, filtering, vsync) and the console's own
settings. Option+Return, F11 or the window's green button: full screen. F3:
frame times, the display's refresh and the sound's lag. On a Mac keyboard
F1, F3 and F11 may need the fn key.

Settings (beatthebeat.ini), saves (nand) and the shader cache are kept in
~/Library/Application Support/qilin/@GAME@, with the folder you chose, the
build (work) and its cache of compiled files (build-cache).


What's in the app
-----------------
Right-click @TITLE@.app, Show Package Contents:

Contents/MacOS/Play @TITLE@     chooses your extracted disc, builds the
                                  game (once) and starts it
Contents/Resources/kit/qilin      the recompiler: turns the disc's @BOOT@
                                  into C++
Contents/Resources/kit/rhf        the Rhythm Heaven Fever decompilation's
                                  function names, which the recompiler
                                  matches to your disc's code
Contents/Resources/kit/lib, include
                                  the prebuilt rest of the game: its
                                  runtime, the renderer, sound, the window
                                  and controls (SDL3), the menu (Dear ImGui)
Contents/Resources/kit/files.txt  every file this release holds (see below)
Contents/Frameworks/libMoltenVK.dylib
                                  MoltenVK: Vulkan over Metal (Khronos)
Contents/Resources/LICENSES       the licences of everything included

The game itself (@EXE@), built from your disc, is in the work folder above,
not in the app: the app is never changed by building.

Source, changes and issues: https://github.com/@REPO@


Updates and recovery
--------------------
"Check for updates" in the app asks GitHub for a newer release (it sends
nothing about you or your Mac). If there is one, it downloads the disk
image, copies the new app beside this one and swaps it in, then starts
again and builds the game for the new version. If anything fails on the
way, the old version is left as it was. The app must be somewhere you can
write to for that (Applications is, for an administrator): run from the
disk image itself, it can't update. You can also drag a new download's app
over this one in Applications: it finds your folder and the cache by
itself. Your saves and settings are never in the app, so nothing of yours
is touched.

The game is compiled for this Mac's processor; on another Mac, it is built
again. Compiled files come from the cache when nothing they're made from has
changed. A failed build leaves the working game as it was; the one before a
new build is kept as work/@EXE@.previous until the new one has run and
closed normally. "Build again from scratch" compiles everything anew.
